00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,450
This lesson develops an interview-ready approach to Design a URL Shortener. The central model is: Map short, globally

2
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:34,950
unique keys to destination URLs under read-heavy traffic. Begin by clarifying the product behavior rather than naming technologies.

3
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:52,450
Ask who uses the system, what the critical write and read paths are, which operations must be correct,

4
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:09,950
and which outcomes may be delayed. State two or three non-goals. This prevents a familiar architecture from being

5
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:27,450
forced onto the wrong product. Separate functional requirements from quality attributes. Functional requirements describe user-visible actions and state

6
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:44,950
changes. Quality attributes describe scale, latency, availability,

7
00:01:45,000 --> 00:02:02,450
durability, consistency, privacy, and cost. Pick the two attributes that drive architecture and say why. Avoid promising maximum

8
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:19,950
consistency, maximum availability, minimum latency, and minimum cost simultaneously. A strong interview answer makes the tradeoff visible and

9
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:37,450
ties it to a user consequence. Use rounded assumptions for daily active users, peak requests per second, read-to-write

10
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:54,950
ratio, payload size, retention, and replication. Calculate only values that influence a decision. Label every assumption and keep

11
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:12,450
units on the page. An estimate is useful when it changes partition count, storage tier, cache capacity, or

12
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:29,950
network design. It is not useful when

13
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:47,450
it creates false precision. Add headroom and distinguish average from peak load. Build the design around these decisions:

14
00:03:47,500 --> 00:04:04,950
ID generation, encoding, redirect semantics, caching, abuse controls, and analytics. Draw clients, edge controls, stateless services, authoritative stores,

15
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:22,450
asynchronous paths, and observability. Put arrows in the direction of data flow and label synchronous versus asynchronous work.

16
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:39,950
For each durable write, identify the source of truth. For each cache or index, identify how it becomes

17
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:57,450
fresh enough. For each queue, identify the consumer's idempotency strategy. Define the smallest useful API and the core

18
00:04:57,500 --> 00:05:14,950
data entities. Include identifiers, ownership, timestamps or

19
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:32,450
versions, and uniqueness constraints. Explain the dominant query patterns before selecting storage. If data is partitioned, name the

20
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:49,950
partition key and show why it spreads load without breaking essential queries. If an operation crosses partitions or

21
00:05:50,000 --> 00:06:07,450
services, describe the consistency boundary and how partial completion is reconciled. Pressure-test the design against Hot links, malicious

22
00:06:07,500 --> 00:06:24,950
destinations, key collisions, and expired redirects. Walk through timeout, retry, dependency outage, overload, and recovery. Bound every retry

23
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:42,450
with deadlines, backoff, and jitter. Use idempotency where a repeated request can create a duplicate effect. Define whether

24
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:59,950
the system fails closed, fails open, serves

25
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:17,450
stale data, queues work, or rejects it. Then explain how operators detect the condition and how the system

26
00:07:17,500 --> 00:07:34,950
returns to normal. For every major component, connect the choice to a requirement and name the cost it

27
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:52,450
introduces. A cache reduces latency but creates freshness and invalidation work. Replication improves resilience and read capacity but

28
00:07:52,500 --> 00:08:09,950
introduces lag, conflict, and failover decisions. Partitioning increases scale but complicates queries, transactions, and rebalancing. Asynchronous work absorbs

29
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:27,450
bursts but changes completion semantics and requires retries, deduplication, and backlog operations. This requirement-choice-cost pattern keeps the answer

30
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:44,950
grounded and prevents architecture by buzzword. Start

31
00:08:45,000 --> 00:09:02,450
with the simplest architecture that satisfies the stated scale. Then identify the first bottleneck and evolve one dimension

32
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:19,950
at a time: add caching for read pressure, partition state for size or write throughput, queue noncritical work

33
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:37,450
